{
  "_about": "The invasion's domains (decided 9 October; bible section 9). The other world is a JRPG world and the rift is where its overworld pushes into ours: a domain is one biome of the classic overworld, its bleed (the tileset the flattened ground paints, DomainGround's: plains, sand, marsh or lava), its wildlife with one tactical signature, and its hero. A rift rolls its domain when it opens, one lead and at most one guest. A pack on the battlefield is one composition on one hex: one sprite (its lead's), one badge (its count), its members listed on a tap (the foes' fresh start, 30 October). compositions are the packs the rift places and sends (the domain's shape on the map), each {id, size, tiers, members}: size is patrol (one or two front types, three to five foes, a detachment's fight), formation (a front and a back, six to sixteen by the tier, the body's fight and the unit the Fractures are counted in), elite (led by a foe flagged elite, the guaranteed curveball a Fracture spawns) or escort (a boss's hand-tailored escort at a tier, the tier's units standing in round robin where none is written; one may name its boss, the boss's own coming first: the King Gel's Gels, which its merge takes in, 30 October); tiers lists the tiers (1 to 3) that may deal it, and a tier with none of a size takes the highest tier's under it (every domain needs a tier-one formation and patrol); members are {foe, strength} in order, the first the lead (its sprite and its behaviour; the pack's ring the largest of its members', its pace the slowest, winged only when every member is). Three fields more, each optional (the long wall brief, docs/briefs/long_wall_build.md 3.7 and section 6; no composition writes them yet, the formation pass does in chat): squad, the machines it is intended for, 1 to 4, shown on the map as its threat rating, a patrol reading 1, a formation 3, an elite and an escort 4 where it names none (Ryan, 6 November); layout, {front: [four cells], back: [four cells]} left to right as it stands, each cell null for empty, a foe id, or a list of foe ids the battle's seed draws one from as the pack is dealt, a massive foe in a front cell with the back cell behind it null, every foe it names a member and none named in more cells than its strength, the members it does not place waiting as reinforcements do, and with no layout the heaviest hitters stand first from the left; lead, a member's foe id, the pack's sprite and behaviour, its elite in an elite composition, the first member leading where it names none. The spawn lists by tier are derived (ContentDB.spawn_table: the tier's formations' then patrols' members, each foe once, first seen first) for the harness, the runners and a boss's escort; boss lists the domain's bosses (bible section 9: a named JRPG entity each, three a domain before its hero and more welcome (Ryan, 3 November), each battle drawing one its domain has not ended on this run; the slate's twelve, 30 October, the old massive stand-ins gone to the rank and file and the Bone Wyvern to the elites; the Malboro, the Tonberry and the Adamantoise out of the lists 3 November, species rather than named entities, the Mantis Ant the Meadow's third and the Marsh Serpent the Marsh's fourth since 4 November, the Emperor Scorpion the Waste's third); bench names the heroes the campaign's start may deal to this domain, one per campaign, the domain's final (the party, bible section 9: the hero stands in its rank with the domain's wildlife in the other columns). A domain names no map shape: a battle draws its shape by its seed (Ryan, 30 October: \"I don't see why the domain should be related to the map type in any way\"). Shown to the player: name, flavour. design is internal and never shown: fantasy, signature (what the domain does), answer (how the player beats it, in the engine's own vocabulary). Numbers are placeholders.",
